The active file's truncate lowers its logical end only after ftruncate
succeeds. An ftruncate that cuts the file and then fails leaves the end past
EOF, and reads of published records near the tail fail while the engine is
degraded. Reverts #248's ordering (#236).
The chaos rig cannot catch this one. resume()
cuts below the logical end only when unpublished records have vanished (a
failed fdatasync, then an eviction). An ftruncate that cuts and then fails
there leaves the end stale, but the next resume() - 10 to 110 ms later - scans
to the real end of file, finds nothing left to cut and succeeds, so the stale
end lasts one retry. In that window its only symptom is a reader's I/O error,
which evictions also cause honestly. #248's prove_resume cells, which fail
io_resume_truncate after the cut lands and read every published key while
degraded, are the guard for it. Until #280 they could not fail: the fault
fired after the end was lowered in either order, and now stands in for
ftruncate's own result.
Guarded-by: "*__truncate_fails_after_cut"
diff --git a/bytecaskdb/data_file.cppm b/bytecaskdb/data_file.cppm
index 60dfeeaa..1f708a7c 100644
--- a/bytecaskdb/data_file.cppm
+++ b/bytecaskdb/data_file.cppm
@@ -672,8 +672,6 @@ struct WritableFileOps {
   // past EOF once the cut lands (#246).
   void truncate(Offset new_size) {
     assert(new_size <= logical_end());
-    set_logical_end(new_size);
-    zeroed_end_ = new_size;
     const auto rc = ::ftruncate(fd_, narrow<off_t>(new_size));
     const auto err = errno;
 #ifdef BYTECASK_TESTING
@@ -686,6 +684,8 @@ struct WritableFileOps {
       throw std::system_error{err, std::system_category(),
                               "WritableFileOps::truncate: ftruncate failed"};
     }
+    set_logical_end(new_size);
+    zeroed_end_ = new_size;
   }
 
   // Keeps the file zero-filled ahead of the write cursor: before an append
